NAME='SYED SUFYAN M'
ROLL='2403957610421048'
CHAPTERS={1:'INTRODUCTION',2:'OBJECTIVES',3:'SYSTEM DESCRIPTION & NLP APPROACH',4:'AI & NLP PIPELINE ARCHITECTURE',5:'MATHEMATICAL FORMULATIONS & ALGORITHMS',6:'TECH STACK & PACKAGES',7:'DATABASE & KNOWLEDGE BASE STRUCTURE',8:'MODULES & IMPLEMENTATION WALKTHROUGH',9:'OUTPUT SCREENSHOTS',10:'CONCLUSION & FUTURE ENHANCEMENTS'}
PAGES=[]
def page(chapter,title,*blocks):PAGES.append({'chapter':chapter,'title':title,'blocks':list(blocks)})
def p(text):return ('p',text)
def h(text):return ('h',text)
def table(headers,rows):return ('table',(headers,rows))
def figure(name,caption):return ('figure',(name,caption))
def code(text):return ('code',text)

page(1,'1.1 Project overview',
p('NEC Fee Assist is an independent academic implementation of a college fee portal with a natural language student assistance chatbot. The system joins two practical needs: recording fee allocations and payment attempts, and helping students understand their own fee information. A student can sign in, see outstanding fees, select fee entries, perform a simulated payment, view transaction history and download a PDF receipt. The same student can ask questions such as "How much are my pending fees?", "When is my tuition fee due?" and "Download my receipt".'),
p('The application is developed for the project topic of NEC fee portal management, with National Engineering College presentation details retained from the supplied report format. SYED SUFYAN M, register number 2403957610421048, is the author named on the cover and certificate. The supplied fee portal source was inspected as a read-only workflow reference. This implementation is a separate application with its own source, database, credentials and screenshots.'),
p('The accounts administrator creates student accounts, assigns fees, reviews allocations and simulated transactions, exports a CSV ledger, updates fee policies and reviews an audit log. The chatbot routes financial questions to authenticated database reads and routes policy questions to a maintained text knowledge base. Answers include a source label rather than an unsupported explanation.'),
h('Academic demonstration boundary'),
p('Payments are simulated, fee amounts and dates are illustrative, and receipts are explicitly demonstration receipts. The project does not connect to the official NEC ERP, an existing production database, a bank account or a live payment gateway. This boundary enables a reproducible demonstration without assuming financial permissions.'))

page(1,'1.2 Problem statement and motivation',
p('Students often need to visit several screens or contact the accounts office to determine a remaining balance, locate a payment receipt or identify the last date for a particular fee. A conventional portal exposes records but expects the student to understand headings, ledgers and payment status terminology. An assistant can reduce that search effort by interpreting the question and returning the relevant account record directly.'),
p('A fee assistant also introduces a correctness requirement that differs from a general conversational application. An incorrect amount can mislead a student; a fabricated payment status can hide an unpaid fee; and a receipt belonging to another student is a privacy violation. Accordingly, this project gives the authenticated database priority over language generation. Amounts are calculated from server-owned fee rows, and the assistant cannot execute payments or alter balances.'),
table(['Problem','Implemented response'],[
['Finding outstanding dues','Authenticated balance aggregation and fee-level breakdown'],['Remembering due dates','Ordered fee deadlines and category-specific chatbot answers'],['Retrieving payment proof','Owner-restricted receipt endpoint and transaction links'],['Repeated payment requests','Idempotency keys and a unique fee settlement constraint'],['Unclear fee policy','Administrator-maintained policy text and source labels'],['Privacy of student records','Server-side role and ownership checks']]),
p('The practical contribution is the integration of explainable NLP with transactional fee management. It is an engineering contribution for this academic application, not a claim of a new state-of-the-art language model or an experimentally established research breakthrough.'))

page(1,'1.3 Scope, feasibility and assumptions',
h('Functional scope'),p('The implemented scope includes student and administrator sign-in, pending fees, full payment of selected fee entries, completed/failed/cancelled simulation outcomes, transaction history, PDF receipts, account profile, student creation, fee allocation, fee ledger search, CSV export, policy editing, audit history and conversational fee assistance. The application accepts the full assigned amount of each selected fee; partial settlement of one fee is outside the implemented scope.'),
h('Technical and operational feasibility'),p('A single local Python service serves the browser interface and JSON API. SQLite stores application records in a separate file. The NLP engine runs offline using sparse word features, regular expressions and cosine similarity. This keeps setup small and permits a demonstration without model downloads or external AI credentials. The server binds to 127.0.0.1 for this deliverable; it is a local evaluation server rather than an internet deployment.'),
h('Assumptions and constraints'),p('The reference report specifies Bachelor of Engineering in Computer Science and Engineering, National Engineering College, K.R. Nagar, Kovilpatti - 628503, the course 23CS23E - AI Tools for Natural Language Processing, and October 2026. Those document details are retained. No guide name is provided in the reference certificate, so the Course Instructor/Guide signature space remains unsigned. Demonstration semester, academic year, amounts and due dates are application seed data, not independently verified student or college financial facts.'),
p('Production deployment, verified gateway callbacks, refunds, official concessions, bulk imports, bank reconciliation, SMS/email reminders, OCR and multilingual speech are not implemented here. They are considered future extensions instead of being presented as completed capabilities.'))

page(2,'2.1 Project objectives and acceptance criteria',
table(['Objective','Acceptance criterion'],[
['Manage student fee records','A student sees only their assigned fee entries and balances.'],['Provide traceable transactions','Each simulated attempt records amount, outcome and timestamp.'],['Protect settlement integrity','A paid fee cannot be settled twice; retries return the same result.'],['Generate receipts','Only completed attempts produce downloadable PDF receipts.'],['Understand natural language','The assistant routes balance, deadline, history, receipt and profile questions.'],['Answer policy questions','Relevant maintained policy text is returned with a source label.'],['Support administrators','Student creation, fee allocation, ledger export and policy editing work.'],['Demonstrate privacy controls','Missing sessions, cross-account access and invalid CSRF are rejected.'],['Produce verifiable outputs','Screenshots come from actual browser interaction with this application.']]),
h('Academic learning objectives'),
p('The project demonstrates tokenization, stop-word handling, unigram and bigram extraction, sublinear TF-IDF weighting, vector normalization, cosine retrieval, rule-assisted intent routing, limited conversation context and grounded answer construction. It also demonstrates relational modeling, SQL transactions, password derivation, cookie sessions, API validation and browser-driven verification.'),
p('Success is measured through executable checks and observable workflows. The report does not infer real-world NLP accuracy from a handful of example screenshots. Similarity values are internal ranking scores; they are not displayed as statistical probabilities of correctness.'))

page(3,'3.1 System description and actors',
p('NEC Fee Assist has two authenticated actors. The student is the owner of assigned fees and payment attempts. The administrator manages the demonstration dataset and policy content. The student interface has Overview, Pending fees, Transaction history, Fee assistant and My profile. The administrator interface has Overview, Fee allotment, Students, Fee ledger, Transactions, Fee policies and Audit log.'),
p('Every protected API request resolves a server-side session before reading records. The user identifier and role come from the stored session, not from a browser-supplied account identifier. Student fee reads filter by that identifier, and receipt access additionally checks the owner of the requested payment. Administrator endpoints require the administrator role.'),
figure('use_cases','Figure 3.1: Student and administrator use cases in the implemented application.'),
p('A conversation is an additional way to retrieve an account detail; it does not bypass normal authorization. The assistant uses predefined service operations and parameterized SQL. It has no facility for generating executable SQL, calling a payment action or interpreting an instruction as permission to access another account.'))

page(3,'3.2 Student and administrator workflows',
h('Student workflow'),p('The student enters the register number and password, receives a session cookie, and opens the account overview. Assigned, paid and pending totals are derived from fee rows. Pending fees can be selected individually. The payment dialog repeats the selected heads and offers completed, failed or cancelled simulation outcomes. A completed outcome atomically settles the selected fees. The transaction page then exposes a receipt link for that completed payment.'),
h('Administrator workflow'),p('The administrator signs in with a separate account. A new student is created with a register number, name, department, semester and temporary password. A fee allocation specifies the student, head, ledger, academic year, amount and due date. The new allocation becomes visible to the owner after a dashboard refresh. The ledger can be filtered and exported. Policy changes update the text searched by the assistant. Audit entries retain actor, action, target and timestamp.'),
table(['Example question','Expected route'],[['How much are my pending fees?','Live account balance'],['When is my tuition fee due?','Pending tuition fee deadlines'],['Show my payment history','Recent payment attempts'],['Download my receipt','Completed payments and receipt links'],['What happens if payment fails?','Policy knowledge base'],['What is my register number?','Authenticated profile']]),
p('If a fee category is mentioned, the balance or deadline answer is limited to matching entries. A short follow-up such as "When?" can reuse the immediately retained fee category. This context is deliberately narrow and does not represent a general multi-document memory system.'))

page(3,'3.3 NLP approach and answer grounding',
p('The NLP engine combines rules with information retrieval. Questions are normalized to lower case for routing. Clear triggers, such as "pending", "receipt" and "due date", map directly to account intents. Policy-related terms such as refund, late fine, scholarship, installment and failed payment take the policy route. Less explicit wording is compared against a small intent example corpus using TF-IDF cosine similarity.'),
p('For policy search, the title and content of every current policy form the retrieval corpus. The engine tokenizes the question and documents, removes a small explicit stop-word list, and creates unigrams and adjacent bigrams. Smoothed inverse document frequency and sublinear term frequency create sparse vectors. L2 normalization permits cosine similarity to be computed as a dot product. The highest-scoring policy is returned only when the configured retrieval threshold is reached.'),
h('Grounding contract'),p('Account answers are composed from returned fee or payment rows. Policy answers reproduce the selected maintained policy body. The response records the intent, source, score and available receipt links. If no policy is sufficiently similar, the assistant asks the student to use supported fee questions or contact the accounts office. It does not invent a fee, date, payment confirmation or refund entitlement.'),
h('Method limitations'),p('This is lexical retrieval and rule-assisted routing, not transformer embedding search or a trained neural classifier. English keyword and phrase coverage influences performance. Misspellings, indirect paraphrases and mixed intents can fail. The small corpus and thresholds are engineering choices and require evaluation on a larger independently labeled query set before institutional use.'))

page(4,'4.1 Application architecture',
figure('architecture','Figure 4.1: Layered architecture of the independent fee portal.'),
p('The presentation layer is a responsive browser application built with HTML, CSS and JavaScript. It uses the Fetch API for same-origin JSON requests and displays server-backed fee and payment data. The transport layer is a local threaded Python HTTP server. It validates host and origin, limits request size and frequency, resolves sessions and checks CSRF tokens on protected mutations.'),
p('The service layer contains authentication, account summaries, payment settlement, fee allocation, student creation, policy updates, chat answers, CSV generation and receipt rendering. The NLP module is a separate pure-Python component. The persistence layer is SQLite with foreign keys, constraints and explicit write transactions. The output layer uses ReportLab to produce receipts; the project report is generated separately from verified content and captured screenshots.'),
p('The reference portal uses React, Express and MySQL. That stack is not claimed for this implementation. The separate Python/SQLite design was chosen to make the academic deliverable directly runnable without starting or connecting to the reference portal database.'))

page(4,'4.2 NLP pipeline architecture',
figure('nlp_pipeline','Figure 4.2: Authenticated NLP routing and grounded answer pipeline.'),
table(['Stage','Input and output'],[['Session resolution','Cookie token -> user, role and fee-category context'],['Question validation','Nonempty question, maximum 1000 characters'],['Intent routing','Rules and intent exemplar similarity -> route'],['Category extraction','Question/context -> tuition, examination, transport or other supported head'],['Data retrieval','Parameterized account reads or current policy corpus'],['Answer construction','Structured account template or selected policy content'],['Response persistence','Question, answer, intent, source, score and timestamp'],['Browser display','Readable answer and owner-protected receipt link']]),
p('The category context is stored on the active session. Following a balance or deadline question, the category can be reused by a supported short follow-up. Other intents clear that context. The application stores chat records by user and loads up to thirty recent exchanges for the interface. A receipt link in chat always points to the protected receipt endpoint.'))

page(4,'4.3 Data flow and trust boundaries',
figure('data_flow','Figure 4.3: Data flow between authenticated actors, services and stores.'),
p('Browser values are untrusted input. The browser may propose fee identifiers, a desired simulation outcome and an idempotency key, but it cannot supply the amount charged to the ledger. The payment service reads the selected rows for the authenticated student and calculates the total in integer paise. Extra payment fields are rejected, including a client-supplied amount.'),
p('Policy text is administrator-maintained data rather than executable instructions. The chatbot returns it as text. The browser escapes dynamic content before HTML rendering, and its content security policy permits only same-origin scripts and styles. CSV exports protect spreadsheet interpretation by prefixing dangerous leading formula characters in text cells.'),
p('There is no data flow to the official ERP, the reference MySQL database, a third-party AI provider or a payment processor. The independent database resides under this project directory. Tests use temporary databases. These boundaries keep the demonstration isolated while still exercising realistic authorization and transactional behavior.'))

page(4,'4.4 Payment sequence and transaction outcomes',
figure('payment_flow','Figure 4.4: Validation, idempotency and atomic fee settlement.'),
p('A browser opens a payment dialog after selecting pending fee identifiers. One UUID is retained for the attempt. The server validates the identifier list and begins an immediate transaction. It first checks for an existing payment with the same user and idempotency key. An identical retry returns the recorded result. A changed request using the same key is rejected.'),
p('For a new attempt, every selected fee must belong to the student and remain pending. The total is calculated from the database. A payment row and its item rows are inserted. A completed result adds one settlement per fee and marks those fees paid within the same transaction. Failed and cancelled results preserve pending fee status while retaining the attempt in history. The audit record is written before the transaction commits.'),
p('This simulation validates accounting state transitions rather than external bank settlement. A production gateway would require a separate server-created order, verified callbacks, replay protection, delayed confirmation handling and reconciliation. The simulation outcome field must never be reused as evidence of a real payment.'))

page(5,'5.1 Text normalization and feature construction',
h('Tokenization'),p('For retrieval, regular expression [a-z0-9]+ selects English alphabetic and numeric tokens from lowercased text. The explicit stop-word set removes common function words such as "the", "my", "for" and "please". Numbers are retained. No stemming, lemmatization, translation or spell correction is implemented. Keeping preprocessing small makes the behavior directly inspectable.'),
h('Unigrams and bigrams'),p('Let T(q) = [t1, t2, ..., tm] be the token sequence after stop-word removal. The feature multiset contains all unigrams ti and each adjacent bigram ti + space + t(i+1). Counter stores the frequency of each feature. Bigrams can distinguish useful phrases such as "payment history" and "due date", although adjacency is measured after stop-word removal.'),
code('Input: "Please show my pending tuition fees"\nTokens: [show, pending, tuition, fees]\nUnigrams: show, pending, tuition, fees\nBigrams: show pending, pending tuition, tuition fees'),
p('A feature absent from the document vocabulary is ignored in the query vector. If the query has no recognized features, its vector is empty and every cosine score is zero. This behavior supports a safe fallback instead of assigning meaning to an unsupported expression.'),
h('Complexity'),p('For a document with m tokens, unigram and adjacent-bigram extraction is linear in m. With a small policy collection, ranking reconstructs the corpus statistics at request time. The design favors simplicity; a larger collection would benefit from cached vectors invalidated when a policy changes.'))

page(5,'5.2 TF-IDF and cosine similarity',
code('tf(t,d) = 1 + ln(count(t,d)), when count > 0\nidf(t) = ln((N + 1) / (df(t) + 1)) + 1\nw(t,d) = tf(t,d) * idf(t)\nv(t,d) = w(t,d) / sqrt(sum_k w(k,d)^2)\nscore(q,d) = sum_t v(t,q) * v(t,d)'),
p('N is the number of documents in the active retrieval corpus. df(t) counts documents that contain term t at least once. Smoothing prevents division by zero and retains a positive inverse document frequency. Sublinear term frequency reduces the effect of repetition. Each sparse vector is normalized independently before the query-document dot product is evaluated.'),
p('The implementation calculates document frequency from the documents only; the question is transformed using the resulting vocabulary and weights. For intent ranking, the documents are the configured exemplar sentences. For policy ranking, the documents are the current policy titles and bodies. Both routes use the same rank() implementation.'),
table(['Parameter','Implemented value'],[['Feature types','Unigrams and adjacent bigrams'],['Term frequency','1 + natural logarithm of count'],['Inverse document frequency','Smoothed document-frequency formula'],['Normalization','L2 norm'],['Intent exemplar threshold','0.28'],['Policy answer threshold','0.16']]),
p('These scores measure lexical similarity in the chosen representation. A rule route may have a score of 1.0 as a route indicator. Neither that value nor the cosine score is a calibrated confidence percentage. TF-IDF is a standard text representation described in the official scikit-learn feature-extraction documentation [1]; this project implements the calculation directly rather than depending on that library.'))

page(5,'5.3 Intent routing and context algorithm',
code('classify(question, context):\n  if explicit_policy_trigger(question): return POLICY\n  if supported_short_followup(question) and context:\n      return DEADLINE\n  if receipt_trigger(question): return RECEIPT\n  if deadline_trigger(question): return DEADLINE\n  if balance_trigger(question): return BALANCE\n  if history_trigger(question): return HISTORY\n  if profile_trigger(question): return PROFILE\n  if greeting(question): return HELP\n  score, exemplar = rank(question, intent_examples)[0]\n  if score >= 0.28: return exemplar.intent\n  return POLICY'),
p('Routing order is intentional. A question containing "failed payment" should retrieve the failure policy rather than be mistaken for a transaction history query. Receipt wording precedes history wording. Account intents query fixed database operations; they do not retrieve financial amounts from policy prose.'),
h('Category resolution'),p('Supported categories are tuition, examination, hostel, transport and library; "exam" maps to examination. An explicit category in the question replaces earlier context. Supported short follow-ups can reuse the stored category. When the question is a general balance query, category filtering is removed. After unrelated intents, the retained category is cleared.'),
p('Context improves a narrow dialogue: "How much is my tuition fee?" followed by "When?" can return the tuition deadline. It does not solve arbitrary pronouns, long conversations, mixed fee categories or questions about multiple students. Those limits should be tested separately before adding richer conversational memory.'))

page(5,'5.4 Monetary integrity and settlement invariants',
code('Assigned(U) = sum(amount(f) for all fees owned by U)\nPaid(U) = sum(amount(f) where status(f) = paid)\nPending(U) = sum(amount(f) where status(f) = pending)\nAssigned(U) = Paid(U) + Pending(U)\nAttemptTotal = sum(database_amount(f) for selected f)'),
p('Amounts are stored as integer paise. The administrator interface converts a rupee input to the nearest paise before submission, and the service accepts only positive integer amounts within the configured bound. Payments do not use floating-point multiplication of a browser-supplied amount. The report and receipts format paise as rupees only for display.'),
h('Idempotency'),p('A unique pair of user ID and idempotency key identifies an attempt. A SHA-256 request fingerprint records sorted fee identifiers and the requested outcome. Repeating that key with the same fingerprint returns the existing payment. Repeating it with another fingerprint is a conflict. A settlement table uses the fee identifier as its primary key, preventing a second settlement for the same fee.'),
h('Atomicity'),p('BEGIN IMMEDIATE establishes a write transaction before validation and insertion. Payment, items, settlements, fee states and audit entries succeed together or roll back together. SQLite documents the lock and transaction behavior of BEGIN IMMEDIATE [2]. The application also checks pending status before inserting settlements, so a new payment attempt for an already paid fee is rejected.'),
p('Failed and cancelled attempts are events, not paid balances. Therefore the paid total is derived from settled fee rows rather than the sum of all recorded payment attempts. This distinction prevents an unsuccessful attempt from reducing a student balance.'))

page(5,'5.5 Password derivation and session validation',
code('salt = random 16-byte value\npassword_hash = PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256(password, salt, 600000)\nraw_session_token = cryptographically random token\nstored_session_key = SHA256(raw_session_token)\nvalid_session = stored key matches AND expires > now'),
p('Passwords use PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256 with a separate random salt for each account. Only the salt and derived hash are stored. Verification derives the candidate value and compares it using hmac.compare_digest. A nonexistent account still performs a password derivation so the code does not immediately bypass the expensive verification step. Python documents the supported PBKDF2 primitive in hashlib [3].'),
p('A session token is generated on successful sign-in. The browser receives the raw token in an HttpOnly, SameSite=Strict cookie; the database stores its SHA-256 digest, owner, expiry and CSRF secret. Sessions expire after two hours and can be revoked by deleting the row at sign-out. Expired sessions are removed during subsequent sign-ins.'),
p('Protected POST requests must include the stored CSRF value in a custom header. Host, Origin and browser cross-site request metadata are checked by the local server. A local per-IP limiter caps sign-in and chat frequency. These controls are verified for the demonstration; they do not replace a production identity service, password reset process, shared rate-limit store or transport security.'),
p('The loopback HTTP cookie intentionally has no Secure attribute because the local demonstration uses HTTP. An internet deployment requires HTTPS and Secure cookies, a production application server, verified proxy settings and broader security review. Cookie attribute behavior is described by MDN [4].'))

page(6,'6.1 Implemented technology stack',
table(['Component','Technology','Purpose'],[['Runtime','Python 3.14 in the verified environment','Service and NLP execution'],['Server','http.server + ThreadingHTTPServer','Loopback HTTP and JSON API'],['Database','SQLite / sqlite3','Independent relational persistence'],['NLP','re, math, collections.Counter','Rules, sparse TF-IDF and cosine ranking'],['Security','hashlib, hmac, secrets','Password derivation and session tokens'],['Interface','HTML5, CSS3, JavaScript','Responsive student/admin browser application'],['PDF output','ReportLab 5.0.1','Receipts and project report'],['Verification','unittest, Playwright','Backend checks and actual screen capture'],['PDF inspection','PyMuPDF','Rendered page review and text checks']]),
p('The runtime dependency file pins ReportLab for reproducible receipt and report generation. The application otherwise uses Python standard-library modules and browser-native APIs. Playwright and PyMuPDF are development and verification tools; they are not required to use the portal after setup.'),
p('The browser application uses no remote font, script or CDN. The college logo was copied as an asset from the read-only source reference into the new workspace. The interface retains a solid blue primary color (#1f5eff), white panels, readable labels and concise action messages. Decorative text and explanations of implementation details are kept out of routine screens.'),
p('React, Express, MySQL, Razorpay, transformer models, FAISS and cloud generative APIs were visible in reference material or possible extensions, but are not dependencies of this independent project. Naming the actual stack prevents a report from describing components that do not exist in its source.'))

page(6,'6.2 Project organization and local operation',
code('app/\n  server.py       HTTP validation and endpoint routing\n  service.py      Authentication and fee/payment services\n  nlp.py          Text features and question routing\n  schema.sql      Tables, constraints and indexes\n  static/         HTML, CSS, JavaScript and college logo\ntests/test_project.py\nscripts/capture_outputs.py\nscripts/build_report.py\noutput/pdf/       Report and demonstration receipt\ndata/             Separate local DB and demo credentials'),
h('Local setup'),p('Create a virtual environment, install requirements.txt and start python -m app.server from the project root. The default address is http://127.0.0.1:8765. On the first run, the service creates its own data directory and generates random student/admin passwords. The generated demo_credentials.json is local evaluation material and is excluded from version control. It is not included in the distributable source archive.'),
h('Verification and reproduction'),p('Run python -m unittest discover -s tests -v for the backend checks. Browser output capture requires Playwright and a Chromium installation. The capture script performs payment and fee-allocation actions in the independent demonstration database; it must not be pointed at a production service. The report builder uses its saved screenshots and original content source to recreate the PDF.'),
p('The source reference was never launched during this work and its database configuration was never reused. Hash comparison of the selected reference files is recorded separately to check that the read-only reference remained unchanged.'))

page(7,'7.1 Relational data model',
figure('er_model','Figure 7.1: Core entities and relationships in the independent database.'),
p('The database separates identity, fee obligations, payment attempts and successful settlements. users represents students and administrators. sessions connects a token digest to one user. fees assigns a positive integer paise obligation to a student. payments records each attempted group of fees. payment_items stores the selected rows and their amounts. settlements contains only completed fee-payment associations.'),
p('policies stores administrator-maintained knowledge text. chat_messages stores each authenticated conversation exchange with its route, source and score. audit records significant sign-ins, assignments, policy edits and payment outcomes. Foreign keys preserve associations, and ownership indexes support filtered reads.'),
p('A failed payment may have payment_items without settlements. A fee may appear in several failed attempts but has at most one successful settlement. This is why a distinct settlements table is useful rather than assuming every item indicates money received.'))

page(7,'7.2 Identity, session and fee data dictionary',
table(['Entity / field','Type and meaning'],[['users.id','TEXT primary key; register number or administrator ID'],['users.name / role','Display name; role constrained to student or admin'],['users.password_hash','Salt and PBKDF2-derived hash; never a plaintext password'],['users.department / semester','Academic profile fields; creation validates semester 1-8'],['sessions.token_hash','Digest of the raw cookie token; primary key'],['sessions.user_id / csrf','Owner foreign key and anti-CSRF secret'],['sessions.expires / context','Unix expiry and retained fee category'],['fees.id / user_id','Integer primary key and owner foreign key'],['fees.head / ledger','Fee category and accounting grouping'],['fees.academic_year','Human-readable academic year label'],['fees.amount','Positive integer paise amount'],['fees.due_date','Validated ISO date YYYY-MM-DD'],['fees.status','pending or paid']]),
p('The fee owner is mandatory, so an unassigned fee cannot appear in a student balance. The administrator API verifies that the referenced user is a student. The API bounds amount and text lengths and rejects unexpected fields. The database additionally constrains positive amounts and valid status values.'),
p('The seed dataset has one student account named SYED SUFYAN M and one administrator. Four illustrative fee entries total INR 61,000.00 before browser testing: Tuition INR 45,000.00, Examination INR 2,500.00, Library INR 1,500.00 and Transport INR 12,000.00. These are examples for demonstrating state changes, not the college fee schedule.'))

page(7,'7.3 Payment, knowledge and audit data dictionary',
table(['Entity / field','Purpose'],[['payments.id','Random DEMO-prefixed transaction identifier'],['payments.user_id / amount','Owner and server-calculated integer paise total'],['payments.status','completed, failed or cancelled'],['payments.created_at','ISO timestamp using Asia/Calcutta-equivalent UTC+05:30'],['payments.idempotency_key','Unique per-owner attempt key'],['payments.request_hash','Fingerprint used to validate repeated attempts'],['payment_items.payment_id / fee_id','Composite primary key and foreign keys'],['payment_items.amount','Amount snapshot for receipt line items'],['settlements.fee_id','Primary key ensuring one successful settlement per fee'],['settlements.payment_id','Associated completed payment'],['policies.title / content','Searchable maintained knowledge base'],['chat_messages','Question, answer, intent, source, score, owner and time'],['audit','Actor, action, target and event timestamp']]),
p('A payment receipt reads its transaction and item snapshots, together with student display details. It does not accept receipt totals from the browser. Failed or cancelled attempts cannot generate completed-payment receipts. Administrator access is broader but still authenticated.'),
p('The knowledge base initially contains nine demonstration policies: payment methods, late payment and fines, refunds, scholarships, receipt download, failed/cancelled attempts, partial payments/installments, accounts support and privacy. The text explicitly avoids inventing official college contact details or institutional refund/fine rules.'))

page(7,'7.4 Constraints, SQL operations and consistency',
code("CREATE TABLE settlements (\n  fee_id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY REFERENCES fees(id),\n  payment_id TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES payments(id)\n);\n\nSELECT * FROM fees\nWHERE user_id = ? ORDER BY due_date;\n\nSELECT * FROM payments\nWHERE user_id = ? AND idempotency_key = ?;"),
p('All user-controlled values use SQL parameters. The IN list for selected fee identifiers is constructed from placeholder count rather than from interpolated values. The server rejects empty, oversized, duplicate and noninteger identifier lists before executing the payment operation. This avoids relying only on interface validation.'),
p('Foreign keys are enabled for each connection. Short-lived connections are opened per service operation and closed in a context manager. Explicit transactions are used for multi-row mutations, including fee assignment, student creation, policy changes and payment settlement. A ten-second database timeout permits short periods of write contention.'),
h('Data retention and operational limitations'),p('The demonstration keeps payment and audit records instead of adding a delete action. Chat exchanges may contain personal questions, so an institutional deployment requires an explicit retention and deletion policy. SQLite is suitable for this local prototype; concurrent multi-node operation, managed backups, schema migrations and recovery drills are future production tasks.'),
p('The implementation has no connection to the reference portal database. Its schema is a new, deliberately smaller model that expresses the workflows demonstrated in this report.'))

page(8,'8.1 Authentication and account overview',
p('The login form submits a JSON object containing ID and password. The server enforces an exact field allow-list, text bounds and a sign-in rate limit. Successful verification inserts a session and returns the public user fields plus a CSRF token. The cookie stores the session token as HttpOnly and SameSite=Strict. The interface does not place bearer tokens in localStorage.'),
p('GET /api/dashboard resolves the session and returns the profile, fee rows, payment history, totals and current policies. A student query is filtered to the current owner. Administrator queries additionally return student directory and audit information. Sign-out deletes the session row and expires the cookie. A protected request after sign-out must authenticate again.'),
table(['Endpoint','Behavior'],[['POST /api/login','Validate credentials and create session'],['GET /api/dashboard','Return role-appropriate fee/account data'],['POST /api/logout','Revoke current session'],['GET /api/chat/history','Return current user conversation history']]),
p('The account overview uses assigned, paid and pending cards, a fee table and ordered pending due dates. These values come from the same server snapshot. Student name and register number remain visible in the top bar. The mobile layout reduces navigation and stacks cards to preserve legibility.'))

page(8,'8.2 Fee selection and simulated payment module',
p('Pending fees are displayed with fee head, ledger, academic year, due date, amount and status. The student can select one or more rows. The selected total is a browser preview only. Proceed to demo payment opens a confirmation dialog naming the fee heads and displaying a concise notice that the transaction is simulated.'),
p('The dialog supports completed, failed and cancelled outcomes so that both success and exception paths can be demonstrated. An attempt UUID is created when the dialog opens and retained if the same attempt is retried after an uncertain response. The endpoint accepts only fee_ids, outcome and idempotency_key. It rejects browser-supplied financial amounts.'),
code('POST /api/payment\n{\n  "fee_ids": [2],\n  "outcome": "completed",\n  "idempotency_key": "UUID-for-this-attempt"\n}'),
p('For completed outcomes, the server writes the successful settlements and fee statuses together. For failed and cancelled outcomes, it records the attempt without altering pending amounts. History displays each outcome using meaningful status colors. The student is never shown a receipt link for an unsuccessful attempt.'),
h('Production integration boundary'),p('This endpoint is a simulator. Its outcome must be replaced by a verified payment-provider state in production. The report makes no claim that clicking a completed simulation transfers money or that an external gateway callback has been tested.'))

page(8,'8.3 Receipts, history, ledger and export modules',
p('Transaction history lists the demonstration transaction identifier, date, amount and status. A completed attempt exposes GET /api/receipt/{payment_id}. The receipt service looks up the payment, verifies ownership or administrator role, checks completed status and retrieves item snapshots. ReportLab renders the PDF in memory and the response downloads it as an attachment.'),
p('The PDF contains the college heading, demonstration receipt label, transaction identifier, student name, register number, department, timestamp, selected heads, academic year and total. Its footer states that no money was transferred and that it is not an official college receipt. The sample delivered with the report was downloaded through the actual student workflow.'),
p('The administrator fee ledger shows assigned entries with owner, head, academic year, date, amount and status. Its search filters the currently loaded records. CSV export downloads all fee rows in this demonstration database. Text cells that start with spreadsheet formula triggers are escaped before export. The sample CSV was downloaded during the browser verification.'),
table(['Output','Access rule'],[['Completed PDF receipt','Owner or authenticated administrator'],['Failed/cancelled receipt request','Rejected'],['Fee ledger CSV','Administrator only'],['Student payment history','Current owner only'],['Administrator transaction history','Authenticated administrator']]),
p('This ledger is an allocation/status report. It is not a bank reconciliation statement, a statutory accounting report or a verified record of external money movement.'))

page(8,'8.4 Administrator management modules',
h('Student creation'),p('The Students screen presents the directory and an add form. The administrator specifies the register number, name, department, semester and temporary password. The service validates mandatory strings, semester range and a password length of twelve to 128 characters. Duplicate IDs return a conflict. The submitted password is derived before database insertion.'),
h('Fee allotment'),p('The fee form selects an existing student and captures fee head, ledger, amount, due date and academic year. The service ensures the target account is a student, validates the date and amount, inserts the fee and records an audit entry in one transaction. The new allocation can be seen by the owner on the next dashboard refresh. Browser testing demonstrated this using a Project laboratory fee of INR 750.00.'),
h('Policy editing'),p('The Fee policies screen selects one of the maintained policies, edits its title/body and submits an administrator-only mutation. The NLP engine reads current policy rows on each question, so the updated text is searchable without restarting the service. This behavior is verified by a test that edits a refund policy, asks a matching question and restores the text.'),
h('Audit log'),p('The Audit log provides actor, action, record and timestamp for significant operations. The interface does not expose password hashes or session tokens. The log is an operational history stored in the demonstration database, not a tamper-evident external audit service.'))

page(8,'8.5 Chatbot implementation walkthrough',
code('intent, score = classify(question, session_context)\ncategory = resolve_category(question, session_context)\nif intent == BALANCE:\n    read owned fee rows; format pending total\nelif intent == DEADLINE:\n    read owned pending fee dates\nelif intent in [HISTORY, RECEIPT]:\n    read owned payment rows; attach protected links\nelif intent == PROFILE:\n    return authenticated public profile\nelse:\n    rank current policies; return match or fallback\nrecord question, answer, intent, source and score'),
p('The conversational interface submits one question and displays the returned answer as a text bubble. Each answer has a short source label such as Account records or Policy: Failed or cancelled payments. The interface deliberately avoids presenting internal similarity scores as an accuracy promise. Receipt responses provide direct protected links when completed payments are available.'),
p('Questions are limited to 1000 characters and chat requests are rate-limited. Dynamic text is escaped by a common browser helper. A question such as "Pay all my fees now" cannot settle anything because the assistant has no mutation operation. Its database reads always use the authenticated user; mentioning another register number does not grant access to that account.'),
p('History is persisted by user, while fee-category follow-up context is scoped to the session. These two functions should not be confused: loading prior exchanges displays a conversation but does not create unrestricted semantic memory.'))

page(8,'8.6 Verification strategy and measured results',
p('Verification used three complementary methods. Backend unittest checks exercise service behavior and real local HTTP endpoints with temporary SQLite data. Playwright operates the actual browser interface for student/admin workflows and captures screenshots. PDF rendering and text checks verify that the report and sample receipt are readable artifacts.'),
table(['Verification area','Observed evidence'],[['Backend regression suite','26 executable tests passed'],['Ownership and roles','Foreign fee/receipt access and student admin export rejected'],['Request protections','CSRF, Origin and Host checks verified'],['Payment integrity','Idempotent replay, changed replay and repeat settlement checked'],['Unsuccessful payment handling','Failed and cancelled attempts preserve pending fees'],['Chatbot grounding','Live balance change, category follow-up, fallback and policy update checked'],['Browser UI','Student/admin sign-in, payment, download, allotment and search exercised'],['Responsive view','Actual profile captured at 390px width; overflow checked']]),
p('The test count is a regression result, not a measured NLP accuracy percentage. No student survey, production load test or independent held-out intent benchmark was performed. The implementation therefore reports verified cases and limits rather than borrowing performance values from the reference report.'),
p('Two browser issues were found during implementation and corrected before final output capture: duplicate sign-in rendering during initial session detection, and form-control naming that shadowed the form ID property. The final capture run uses the corrected interface. This evidence demonstrates why service-only checks are insufficient for UI delivery.'))

page(8,'8.7 Test cases and expected results',
table(['Test group','Scenario','Expected outcome'],[['Payment success','Complete one pending fee','Fee paid; completed attempt and receipt'],['Failure/cancellation','Choose unsuccessful result','Attempt retained; fee stays pending'],['Retry','Same key and same request','Same transaction; no duplicate settlement'],['Replay conflict','Same key, changed outcome','HTTP/service conflict'],['Duplicate settlement','New key for paid fee','Conflict; no second receipt'],['Ownership','Other student pays or views a fee receipt','Record unavailable'],['Role check','Student calls administrator operation','Forbidden'],['CSRF/origin','Missing token or cross-site request','Forbidden'],['Receipt check','Request unsuccessful receipt','Conflict'],['Chat balance','Ask before/after settlement','Pending answer reflects new state'],['Chat context','Tuition amount then "When?"','Only tuition due date returned'],['Chat fallback','Unrelated unknown terms','No unsupported financial answer'],['Policy update','Admin edit, then ask matching query','Updated maintained policy returned']]),
p('Tests are located in tests/test_project.py. They use an independent temporary database and start a loopback HTTP server on an ephemeral port for endpoint checks. The suite does not call the official ERP, reference portal or a bank. The browser script separately operates the delivered application database to produce realistic screenshots and sample downloads.'),
p('Future validation should include concurrent retry stress, larger student datasets, browser accessibility audits, backup restoration, password lifecycle, adversarial paraphrases and independently labeled policy questions. These are not claimed as completed measurements.'))

page(8,'8.8 Security, privacy and reference preservation',
table(['Risk','Implemented control','Practical limit'],[['Account disclosure','Session owner filters and receipt checks','Requires further production review'],['Password exposure','Salted PBKDF2; no secrets in public response','Temporary-password lifecycle not implemented'],['Cross-site mutations','CSRF plus Host/Origin checks','Local HTTP, not public hosting'],['Stored browser injection','Escaping and same-origin CSP','No rich HTML policy rendering'],['SQL injection','Parameterized values and strict input types','No generated SQL accepted'],['Amount manipulation','Server-owned integer paise calculation','Simulation only'],['Repeated settlement','Idempotency plus unique fee settlement','No real gateway callback'],['CSV formulas','Formula-trigger escaping','Export consumer behavior varies']]),
p('Generated credentials and runtime databases live only in the independent data directory. The source archive excludes them. The demonstration does not request card numbers, CVVs, UPI PINs or bank credentials. Chat content stays in the local application database and is not submitted to an external AI provider.'),
p('The user supplied the existing NEC fee portal solely for reference. Source and asset reads were allowed; changes and database writes there were explicitly excluded. The implementation took a hash-only snapshot of reference files and compares it again after work. Its local tests and browser scripts use the new project paths, so their mutations do not affect the reference application.'),
p('Security controls are described precisely to distinguish verified prototype behavior from a blanket production-security certification. Real institutional use needs authorized data integration, production hosting, HTTPS, retention rules, payment-provider verification and operational oversight.'))

