page(10,'10.1 Conclusion',
p('NEC Fee Assist demonstrates a complete local student fee workflow with a conversational retrieval layer. Students can inspect assigned fees, perform simulated payments, review attempts, obtain demonstration receipts and ask questions about their account. Administrators can create students, assign fees, review the ledger, export data and maintain policy content. The original report format is adapted to the fee portal topic, with author details, diagrams and actual application outputs replaced.'),
p('The NLP component is useful because it connects natural language to authoritative account reads and maintained policy text. A balance is calculated from owned fee entries, a deadline comes from recorded dates, and a receipt link still enforces ownership at download time. A policy answer uses a named knowledge-base record. The assistant has no write operations, so an instruction in chat cannot mark a fee paid.'),
p('The engineering design addresses common prototype failures: browser amounts are not trusted, retries are idempotent, completed settlements are unique, unsuccessful attempts do not reduce dues, and receipts require both a valid session and a completed payment. Backend and browser verification provide concrete evidence for the implemented scenarios. They do not establish institutional deployment readiness or general-purpose language understanding.'),
h('Delivered academic result'),p('The deliverable contains the separate working source, local run instructions, regression tests, original report content, architecture and database diagrams, captured student/admin screens, a demonstration receipt and a sample fee ledger. Its transactions remain simulated and its seed fees remain illustrative. The read-only reference portal is preserved.'))

page(10,'10.2 Future enhancements and production roadmap',
table(['Priority','Enhancement','Required work'],[['1','Real payment gateway','Server orders, signature verification, callbacks and reconciliation'],['1','Institutional identity integration','Authorized student import, account lifecycle and recovery'],['1','Production hosting','HTTPS, Secure cookies, production server and operational monitoring'],['1','Financial policy governance','Approved fees, fines, refunds and concession workflows'],['2','Advanced NLP','Labeled query dataset, evaluation, typo handling and semantic retrieval'],['2','Notifications','Authorized email/SMS delivery and student communication preferences'],['2','Bulk fee operations','Validated imports, previews, conflict handling and audit events'],['2','Account reconciliation','Verified gateway/bank data and exception resolution'],['3','Regional language support','Tamil/English query evaluation and accessibility review'],['3','Analytics','Aggregated support trends with privacy-aware retention']]),
p('A production pilot should begin with test-provider payments and a small authorized dataset. Every payment state must be derived from verified provider evidence rather than the current simulator outcome. Reconciliation should cover delayed confirmations and failed callback delivery. Data migration and backup restoration should be tested before expanding access.'),
p('For NLP, start by collecting an independently labeled set of realistic student questions and separating evaluation examples from routing configuration. Measure per-intent precision/recall, policy-answer correctness, fallback quality and account-isolation behavior. Add larger models only if measured retrieval failures justify their operational cost and privacy implications.'))

